# Prefer obvious structure

This is a flip-on-break grid bot. Spend complexity on the trading problem, not on looking sophisticated. Names say what the function does. A function is one step a reader can name. The happy zone stays ordinary data and explicit steps. See happy-zone-tests.mdc.

```typescript
type Grid = { readonly breakPrice: number; readonly flipped: boolean };

// ❌ Dense. The next reader rediscovers the flip rule.
function check(g: Grid, p: number): boolean {
    return p >= g.breakPrice && !g.flipped;
}

// ✅ Named steps. The rule is the structure.
function shouldFlipBuyGrid(grid: Grid, currentPrice: number): boolean {
    const priceReachedBreak = currentPrice >= grid.breakPrice;
    const gridStillOpen = !grid.flipped;
    return priceReachedBreak && gridStillOpen;
}
```

Do not compress a decision into a one-liner to look elegant. Split until each function has one reason to change.
